                <pb facs="1020" n="926" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg926"/>

                <head>No. 561.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Neill to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1886-12-22"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 22, 1886</date>. (Received
                        January 8, 1887.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 189.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge receipt of
                    your communication No. 112, dated November 30, 1886, as being in receipt of
                    dispatch No. 171, of the 28th of October last, from this legation, in reference
                    to an act of the Peruvian Congress, declaring null and void all “interior acts”
                    of the Pierola and Iglesias governments.</p>
                <p>I beg leave to state that the “decree” appeared in “El Comercio” of the 21st
                    instant, signed by the President, Andres A. Caceres, and by Manuel Velarde, the
                    then minister of government, and dated the 26th of October, 1886.</p>
                <p>I have etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard R. Neill.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
